<TopicPage slug="subjects/fashion-design/foundational/fashion-design-research">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fashion design begins before sketching: with research. A collection is an argument about the
    world — a mood, a silhouette, a palette — and the designer's first job is to gather,
    curate and transform the raw material that argument needs. The research book is the industry's
    most revealing artefact: show a designer's book and you can predict the collection.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from reference to collection: the research pipeline" widget="equationStep" config={researchCfg} />

  <h2>Transformation is the design</h2>
  <p>
    Copying a reference is illustration; abstracting one is design. The discipline's classic
    exercise: take a non-fashion image — a beetle's shell, a brutalist stairwell, a 1920s
    photogram — and translate it into structure, seam and surface. Judges and tutors read the
    distance between reference and outcome: too close is pastiche, too far is incoherence. The
    sweet spot is where a designer's handwriting appears.
  </p>

  <h2>Fabric is the design's co-author</h2>
  <p>
    Silhouettes are negotiations with material. A liquid satin wants the bias cut; a stiff duchess
    satin builds architecture; a technical knit shapes itself. Professional practice is therefore
    drape-first: pin cloth on the stand early, let its behaviour edit the sketch, and treat the
    fabric board as half the collection's identity. Students who sketch without swatches design
    garments that cannot exist.
  </p>

  <h2>Design history as a research tool</h2>
  <p>
    Every silhouette returns — the 1920s drop waist, the 1950s New Look, the 1990s slip — so the
    designer who knows the archive knows what the present is quoting. Research into fashion
    history, subculture and the global industry is not academic decoration; it is the difference
    between proposing something new and unknowingly reprising something old.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UAL / London College of Fashion programme model; Sorger &amp; Udale, <em>The Fundamentals of Fashion Design</em>; Quinn, <em>Fashion Futures</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
